  "slug": "metabolic-trait-robustness-microbial-e869",
  "summary": "New benchmarks test temporal and interventional causal discovery on simulated kinetic networks and CRISPR perturbation data, exposing method limits.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is head-to-head evaluation of causal discovery for biological networks under realistic constraints. A bioRxiv preprint benchmarks temporal methods (tMIIC, PCMCI+/LPCMCI, SVAR-FCI) on simulated biochemical kinetic models, covering fully and partially observed settings with latent confounders and unobserved variables. A published BMC Bioinformatics-style comparison evaluates interventional causal structure learning algorithms on synthetic and CRISPR perturbation data for gene regulatory network inference. The emphasis is on partial observability and interventional data rather than new algorithms. Adjacent items are looser fits: LaMe, a joint linear-Gaussian latent mediation framework for effects in high-dimensional exposure-mediator-outcome systems; a tutorial on neural simulation-based inference for cognitive models with intractable likelihoods; and hierarchical dynamical surrogates of trust-game behavior. These share a model-inference orientation but not the network benchmarking focus.",
